{"execute":{"doc":1,"cmd":{"Write":{"PasteMarkdown":{"text":"Shade and soil moisture in urban street trees: evidence from twelve streets\n\nHannah Ostrowski¹ · Kwame Mensah² · Lucía Ferrer¹\n\n¹ Department of Geography, University of Northbridge · ² City Trees Trust, Eastport\n\nAbstract. Street trees cool cities, but many die young because the soil around them dries out in summer. We measured soil moisture at 144 trees on twelve streets over two summers and compared trees whose pits were shaded by buildings for more than half the day with trees in full sun. Pits in shade held on average 23 % more water in July and August, and trees in them grew 18 % more new wood. We discuss what this means for planting plans.\n\nKeywords: urban forestry, soil moisture, shade, street design\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nCities plant street trees for shade, cooler air and wellbeing, yet a large share do not survive their first decade. Drought stress in summer is a leading cause. Where a tree is planted along a street affects how much sun its pit receives, but planting plans rarely take this into account.\n\n# 2. Methods\n\nWe chose twelve streets in Eastport with similar soils, tree species and planting dates. On each street we fitted moisture probes at 30 cm depth beside twelve trees and logged readings every hour from June to September in 2024 and 2025. We classed each pit as shaded or sunny from building shadows modelled for the summer solstice.\n\n# 3. Results\n\n| Measure | Shaded pits | Sunny pits | Difference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Mean soil moisture, July–August | 24.1 % | 19.6 % | +23 % |\n| Days below wilting point | 6 | 19 | −68 % |\n| New wood growth (mm) | 7.4 | 6.3 | +18 % |\n\nShaded pits stayed wetter through both summers, and the gap widened in the dry August of 2025. Trees in sunny pits spent almost three times as many days below the wilting point.\n\n# 4. Discussion\n\nOur results suggest that the sunniest positions along a street need deeper pits, more organic matter or watering in their first five summers. Shade from buildings is not always available, but planting plans can use it where it is.\n\nTwo limits apply. All twelve streets share one soil type, so the effect may be smaller on free-draining sands, and two summers cannot show whether the extra growth lasts. We have kept the probes in place to follow the same trees for a further five years.\n\n# 5. Conclusions\n\nShade from buildings makes a measurable difference to the water available to young street trees. Councils can use simple shadow models, already common in planning, to decide where extra soil volume and summer watering will do the most good.\n\n# Acknowledgements\n\nWe thank the Eastport street tree team for access to the planting records, and the residents who watched over the probes on their doorsteps. The work was funded by the Northbridge Urban Futures Fund (grant UF-2023-117).\n\n# References\n\nFenwick, R. and Adeyemi, T. (2021) Why young street trees die. *Urban Ecology Review*, 14(2), 55–71.\n\nMorales, P. (2019) *Soils for City Trees*. Northbridge: Riverside Academic Press.\n\nOsei, K., Larsen, M. and Byrne, J. (2023) Pit design and tree survival in compacted soils. *Journal of Street Ecology*, 8(1), 12–29.\n\nPrice, D. and Whitfield, A. (2024) Shadow modelling for street design. *Planning Methods*, 31(4), 201–219.\n\nVarga, E. (2020) Water use of young lime and plane trees in paved sites. *Arboricultural Notes*, 46, 88–97.\n"}}}}}
